 "what": "A JOIN between the journals dataset and the heteronym substrate. Attribution is by CREATOR FIELD, not by mention — the same discipline the heteronym crosswalk uses. It answers what neither dataset can alone: where each position's work actually sits, and whether an editor presides over the venue they publish in.",
 "method": {
  "attributed": 1458,
  "unmatched_creator": 28,
  "citation_collapse": "9,992 deposit-level internal citation edges collapsed to venue level by mapping each endpoint to its deposit's journal. Within-venue edges reported separately from cross-venue.",
  "two_joins": "TWO JOINS, because the archive holds two kinds of identity.\n  BYLINE JOIN — creator field. Correct for heteronyms: a position owns a deposit when named as creator.\n  ATTESTATION JOIN — witness, substrate and attestation records. Required for MANTLES, because a mantle is a POSITION and a creator field records an OCCUPANT. MMRS's own specification (machinemediation.org/who/, spec v1.1, 2026-08-14) states it: 'A substrate carries a mantle and can cease to carry it — so the mantle is never the same object as its occupant.' Joining mantles on bylines finds nothing and reports the absence as a fact about the Chorus rather than about the join."
 },
