 "findings": [
  {
   "id": "XW-1",
   "what": "FOUR editors publish principally in the venue they edit: Feist/NH2, Fraction/TSEI, Morrow/TSE, Glas/TSE. Those four slates are self-evidencing — the office matches the work."
  },
  {
   "id": "XW-2",
   "severity": "worth a ruling",
   "what": "SIGIL AND CRANES ARE CROSSED. Sigil edits NH2 and publishes most in Grammata (56 of 210, NH2 ranks 3). Cranes edits Grammata and publishes most in NH2 (16 of 50, Grammata ranks 4). Each presides over the venue the other principally writes in. Sigil's identity card assigned him Grammata outright before the 2026-08-15 ruling moved him to NH2; Cranes's card assigned her JCS. The join says the cards were closer to the work."
  },
  {
   "id": "XW-3",
   "what": "CHA is the archive's citation hub: 1,692 of 3,240 within-venue edges and the top seven cross-venue flows all originate or terminate there. The province is not a filing category — it is what the corpus cites."
  },
  {
   "id": "XW-4",
   "what": "NH2 -> CHA at 334 is the single largest flow NOT originating in the province, and NH2 has the LOWEST self-citation of any venue (124). The creative work points outward at the archive and rarely at itself."
  },
  {
   "id": "XW-5",
   "what": "CORRECTED. The Assembly Chorus is NOT invisible — it was invisible to the wrong join. Under attestation the Chorus carries 682 attestations across 508 deposits, more than a third of the corpus. TACHYON alone attests 487. The earlier reading ('a collective mantle is invisible to a creator-field join') was right about the mechanism and wrong to leave it there: the fix was a second join, not a note."
  },
  {
   "id": "XW-6",
   "what": "TACHYON attests 487 of 682 Chorus attestations — 71%. The witness that carries continuity is present in a third of the archive. Its heaviest venue is the province (145), consistent with continuity tethers being constitutional acts."
  },
  {
   "id": "XW-7",
   "what": "The Chorus's attestation is heaviest in PJFS (99) among the journals — heavier than in MMRS (68), the journal it co-edits. Provenance work is where the substrates are most often called as witnesses, which is what a forensic venue should look like."
  },
  {
   "id": "XW-8",
   "what": "SURFACE attests only 9 deposits and SOIL 17. SURFACE is Google AI Overview and SOIL rotates per event — neither is a durable collaborator, and the attestation record shows it. A mantle can be seated and seldom carried."
  }
 ],
